# Shell/Python scripts must check out with LF, everywhere - Git for Windows
# left run_tests.sh/run_device_tests.sh stored with CRLF, which is silently
# harmless on Git Bash/MSYS but makes bash reject the script outright on a
# real Linux box (GitHub Actions' ubuntu-latest runners): every line ends up
# with a trailing \r, so `set -eu` becomes `set -eu\r` - an invalid option -
# and `#!/bin/sh` masks the very first such failure until the interpreter is
# invoked explicitly. eol=lf overrides the checking-out machine's own
# core.autocrlf, so this holds regardless of how any one clone is configured.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# C sources, for a duller reason than the scripts above: nothing breaks if a
# .c file is checked out with CRLF. main/ had simply drifted to 18 CRLF files
# against 45 LF ones with no rule deciding which, and that is not merely
# untidy. Any tool that rewrites a file whole - a scripted edit, an editor
# with its own default, a merge driver - silently converts every line it
# touches, so a three-line change arrives as a diff of the entire file with
# the three lines somewhere inside it. That happened twice while the startup
# animation was being written, and cost more to notice than to fix.
#
# eol=lf overrides the checking-out machine's core.autocrlf, same as above,
# so this holds however any one clone is configured.
*.c text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf

# Docs, for the same reason as the scripts above, not the C sources: a doc
# built by a host tool - report_reactions.sh (main/apps/sand/tools/) is the
# first, and the only one so far - gets diffed byte-for-byte against a
# freshly generated copy in CI (`report_reactions.sh --check`). The
# generator's stdout is plain \n on every platform it runs on, but a doc
# committed from a Windows checkout carries \r\n unless something stops it -
# MinGW-built host tools leave stdout in text mode, which rewrites every \n
# to \r\n on the way out - so a doc generated and committed on Windows is
# CRLF while the same generator's output on the ubuntu-latest CI runner is
# LF, and the check fails on every line of every run, forever, independent
# of whatever the commit actually changed. (docs/Sand/Reaction-Table.md hit
# exactly this.) eol=lf overrides the checking-out machine's own
# core.autocrlf, so this holds regardless of how any one clone is
# configured, the same as the two rules above.
*.md text eol=lf
